# Link Sharing and Copy-URL Rules

## Status

**Decided by the owner. This is settled — do not relitigate.** The owner has repeatedly asked for one
shared way to share/copy any link, app-wide. Build against it. There is no excuse for a hand-rolled
copy button or a bare "copy" icon anywhere.

## Why this exists

Members are non-technical. When they share something, they need to (1) see exactly what link they're
about to share, (2) open it to check it, and (3) copy it — with clear confirmation that the copy
worked. A naked copy icon that silently writes something to the clipboard, or a "Share" that links to
the app homepage instead of the thing being shared, fails them. This was shipped wrong more than once,
so it is now a hard rule.

## The one shared control (mandatory)

Every share / copy-link affordance MUST use the shared control — never a custom button, a raw
`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, or a raw `window.open` / `Linking.openURL` for sharing:

- **Web:** `ShareLink` — `ctf/packages/web/components/shared/share-link.tsx`.
- **Mobile (React Native):** `ShareLink` — `ctf/packages/mobile/src/components/shared/ShareLink.tsx`.

### What the control must always do

1. **Open a popup**, not copy-on-click-with-no-context. The popup shows:
   - the **full, absolute URL** as selectable text (so the member sees exactly what they'll share);
   - **Open in new tab** (web) / **Open link** (mobile) — opens the URL;
   - **Copy link** — on web, copies the absolute URL and shows clear "Copied!" feedback; on mobile,
     hands off to the OS share sheet (React Native `Share`), which includes Copy plus share-to-app, so
     no extra clipboard dependency is needed.
2. **Share an absolute URL.** A relative path (e.g. `/apps/socket-relay`) is resolved against the
   current origin before sharing — never share or copy a bare relative path.
3. **Point at the specific resource**, never the app homepage. A "share this post" link must carry the
   post's id (e.g. `/apps/socket-relay?request=<id>`), not `/apps/socket-relay`. If the resource has no
   deep-linkable destination yet, build the destination (or the deep-link param) — do not fall back to
   the homepage.
4. **Be accessible** (see `ctf/docs/developer/ACCESSIBLE_FORMS_STANDARD.md`): the trigger announces it
   opens a popup (`aria-haspopup`/`aria-expanded`); the popup is a labeled dialog; focus moves into
   the URL field on open and returns to the trigger on Escape/outside-click; copy feedback is
   announced (`aria-live`). On mobile the equivalent accessibility roles/labels apply.
5. **Never silently no-op.** On web, if the async clipboard API is unavailable, fall back to a hidden
   `textarea` + `execCommand` so the copy still works. On mobile, the OS share sheet is the copy/share
   path, so there is nothing to silently fail.

## Sharing a picture of a screen (mandatory)

A screen that is worth posting somewhere — a report, a list of missions, a set of figures — gets a
**picture of itself**, drawn server-side, and it is handed over through one shared control:
`SharePicture` (`ctf/packages/web/components/shared/share-picture.tsx`). It is the picture
counterpart of `ShareLink` and is built the same way: one control, used everywhere, never forked.

**Why a picture at all.** The screens worth sharing are taller than a phone. Capturing one by hand
means scrolling, several screenshots and a stitch that loses rows at the seams and leaves the
phone's own clock and battery in the middle of something meant to be seen in public.

### One to one, with exactly two exceptions (owner directive, 2026-09-20)

The picture **is** the screen. Not a version of it, not a summary of it, not a cleaner layout of
it — the same figures, the same words, the same order, the same branding. A reader who acts on the
picture and opens the app has to land on what they were shown, or the picture taught them nothing
about the app.

Two things may differ, and nothing else:

1. **Privacy.** A screen may hold something that must not travel: ClickLog's ~11 km area
   coordinates are the worked example. Hide that element with `data-capture-hide` — take the thing
   out, do not redraw the page without it.
2. **The deep link.** The picture gains the screen's own address underneath
   (`https://app.chargingthefuture.com/apps/<slug>`), because a picture travels away from the app
   and has to say where to come back to.

So do not "improve" anything on the way into a picture. If the screen says `+3 pts`, the picture
says `+3 pts` — SkillsHunt scores in points, and swapping in ServiceCredits because a
non-member might find credits more compelling makes the picture disagree with the app (owner
directive, 2026-09-20: *"Keep it as is, points … I need it to be consistent information and
branding."*). If the wording, the date format or the naming is wrong, **fix it on the screen**,
where every member sees it, rather than in a picture only an admin makes.

Branding follows `ctf/docs/BRAND_VOICE_LEXICON.md`: the product is **Skills Economy (SE)**.
"Charging The Future" is the domain, not the product name, and does not head a picture.

### The shape

1. **Capture the screen — do not design a second one.** `captureScreen`
   (`lib/share/capture-screen.ts`) redraws the live node into a canvas. The picture *is* the
   screen, so there is nothing to keep in step and a reader who opens the app finds what they were
   shown (owner directive, 2026-09-20: *"I don't want to design a new set of screens. Tedious for
   me to manage. And then the user is not learning the app."*). Pass the node holding the content,
   not the outermost element, and mark anything that should stay out with `data-capture-hide` —
   the app header, the share control itself, anything private on that screen.
2. **Give it the deep link.** The capture takes a `footer` and draws it under the screen, into
   html2canvas's clone rather than the live DOM, so a picture that travels alone says where to go.
   Use the real deep link (`https://app.chargingthefuture.com/apps/<slug>`), not the homepage.
3. **Show it on the screen that asked for it.** The control puts the picture in the panel under
   the button. It never navigates.
4. **Share is the control.** `navigator.share({ files })` on its own press, so the browser's
   transient activation is fresh. Press-and-hold (or right-click) is named in the note beside it and
   needs no code.

**Drawing it server-side is the older way and is being retired.** Two screens still do it — the
ClickLog trend report and the SkillsHunt missions picture — because their route also decides what
to leave out. `SharePicture` takes either source (`capture` or `url`), but a new screen uses
`capture`.

### Never, each one having shipped and failed

- **Never a plain `<a href download>` to the route.** In the installed app on iOS there is no
  browser chrome, so following it navigates the app's own window to the downloaded file, iOS draws
  its file preview with no back control, and force-closing the app is the only way out (owner
  reports, 2026-08-24 and 2026-09-20).
- **Never `navigator.share` straight after the fetch that drew the picture.** Drawing takes a second
  or two, by which time Safari has expired the activation from the press and refuses with
  `NotAllowedError`.
- **Never a blob-URL anchor as the fallback.** In standalone mode it navigates rather than
  downloading, and a blob URL revoked on the next line is read after the click, which is
  "WebKitBlobResource error 1" on a dead page.
- **Never a "Save the file" button.** Removed 2026-09-20 by owner directive: on a phone it does not
  do what its label says, and Share covers every case. Do not add it back.
- **Never a second design of a screen you could photograph.** A drawn copy drifts from the screen,
  is tedious to keep in step, and teaches a reader nothing about the app they are being invited
  into. Capture the screen instead, and where something must not travel, hide that element rather
  than redrawing the page without it.
- **Never reword, re-unit or re-brand on the way into a picture**, however much better the new
  wording reads. That makes the picture disagree with the app. Change the screen instead.

### For agents

Asked for a picture of a screen? Render it with `SharePicture`, pass it a `capture` that calls
`captureScreen` on the node holding the content, the filename, the screen's accent and surface
tokens, and a note saying what the picture carries. Do not write a new
download control, and do not put a bare `<img>` behind your own buttons — extend `SharePicture` if
something is missing. Worked example of a capture: the GDP screen (`components/gdp/gdp-shell.tsx`). Worked examples of
the older drawn kind, kept because their route decides what to leave out: the SkillsHunt admin
Missions tab (`components/skills-hunt/sha-missions.tsx`) and the ClickLog trends dashboard
(`components/click-log/click-log-trend-image-link.tsx`).

## For agents

- Adding any "Share", "Copy link", or link-out affordance? Use `ShareLink`. If a needed capability is
  missing, extend `ShareLink` — do not fork a one-off.
- Reviewing a diff that adds a copy/share button, a `clipboard.writeText`, or a `window.open`/
  `Linking.openURL` for a user-facing share? That's a defect unless it goes through `ShareLink`.
- A share link that resolves to the app homepage is always wrong — it must target the specific resource.
- Deprecated: the old copy-only `CopyLink` (`ctf/packages/web/components/shared/copy-link.tsx`). Do not
  add new usages; convert any remaining ones to `ShareLink`.
